  {
    "@id": "https://vocab.destiny-evidence.org/health-exposure/DSNY000263",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
      {
        "@value": "Food security"
      },
      {
        "@value": "Food safety"
      },
      {
        "@value": "Nutritional availability"
      },
      {
        "@value": "Food system disruption"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
      {
        "@value": "Disruptions or changes to the availability, accessibility, and safety of food, including impacts on nutrition, foodborne pathogen risk, and agricultural productivity."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
      {
        "@id": "https://vocab.destiny-evidence.org/health-exposure"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
      {
        "@value": "Food supply and safety (Exposure)"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
      {
        "@value": "As atmospheric levels of CO2 increase, the nutritional value (protein and essential minerals) of vital food crops is impacted. Warmer temperatures and more extreme weather can also impair food safety (as climate-sensitive pathogens cause foodborne illness and chemical contamination) and distribution (e.g. by disrupting transportation). Food security (crop yields) can be affected by droughts and other extreme weather events, especially when related to essential crops (e.g. staple foods: cereals, grains, roots/tubers, legumes).\n\nExamples of health impacts: undernutrition, salmonella food poisoning and other foodborne diseases, mycotoxin effects, diarrhoea (E.coli), diarrhoea (V. cholerae).\n"
      }
    ]
  },
