  {
    "@id": "https://vocab.destiny-evidence.org/health-exposure/DSNY000262",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
      {
        "@value": "Air pollution"
      },
      {
        "@value": "Ambient air quality"
      },
      {
        "@value": "Indoor air quality"
      },
      {
        "@value": "Particulate matter exposure"
      },
      {
        "@value": "PM2.5 exposure"
      },
      {
        "@value": "PM10 exposure"
      },
      {
        "@value": "Air pollutants"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
      {
        "@value": "Exposure to outdoor or indoor air containing pollutants — including particulate matter, ozone, nitrogen dioxide, and wildfire smoke — at concentrations harmful to health."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
      {
        "@id": "https://vocab.destiny-evidence.org/health-exposure"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
      {
        "@value": "Air quality (Exposure)"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
      {
        "@value": "Air quality deteriorates with climate change through many pathways. 1. Hotter temperatures result in higher levels of ground-level ozone, wildfires result in increased levels of particulate matter and other air pollutants, and plants produce more pollen and allergens as a result of higher CO2 concentrations in the atmosphere. Increased pollen concentrations in the air also result from spread of certain heat- and drought-resistant plant species due to climate change (e.g. ragweed, Ambrosia artemisiifolia). 2. In addition to releasing CO2, the burning or combustion of fossil fuels releases particulate air pollutants. 3. Household (indoor) air pollution (from combustion of fossil fuels used for cooking and heating) is a major contributor to ambient (outdoor) air pollution. Biomass burning (in agricultural production) also causes air pollution. 4. Include indoor air-pollution only if related to fossil fuel combustion. 5. Radon is considered an air quality related exposure which can occur indoor and outdoor (it is related to open mines and can be exacerbated by heat). 6. There is an increase in sand and dust storms due to climate change, which contributes to air pollution. \n\nExamples of health impacts of this exposure: Exacerbations of asthma and other respiratory diseases (COPD); respiratory allergies; cardiovascular diseases; bacterial meningitis and invasive bacterial disease (linked to airborne dust).\n\n\n\n"
      }
    ]
  },
